'''Mjøsa'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Informasjon om stadnamn |url=https://stadnamn.kartverket.no/fakta/431452 |access-date=2025-07-02 |website=Norgeskart |publisher=[[Norwegian Mapping Authority|Kartverket]] |language=no}}</ref> is the largest lake in [[Norway]] and the [[Lakes in Norway#Deepest lakes|fourth deepest in Norway]] and [[List of lakes by depth|Europe]].<ref name="dybdekart" /> It is located in the southern part of Norway, about {{convert|100|km}} north of the city of [[Oslo]]. Its main [[tributary]] is the river [[Gudbrandsdalslågen]] flowing in from the north; the only [[distributary]] is the river [[Vorma]] in the south. Inflows would theoretically need 5.6 years to fill the lake. With an average depth of about {{convert|150|m}}, most of the lake's volume is under sea level. The average outflow of the lake (measured from 1931 to 1982) is {{convert|316|m3/s|lk=on}} which is about {{convert|9959000000|m3/yr|lk=on}}.<ref name="dybdekart" /> Mjøsa contains about {{convert|56.2|km3|cumi|abbr=on}} of water compared to the {{convert|14.8|km3|cumi|abbr=on}} in the lake [[Røssvatnet]], the second largest lake by volume in Norway.

With a surface elevation of about {{convert|121|m|ft}}, the depth of Mjøsa means that the deepest part of the basin is located approximately {{convert|332|m|ft}} below sea level. This is lower than the deepest point of the sea inlet of [[Kattegat]] and the lower than the vast majority of [[Skagerrak]] off Norway's south coast. Mjøsa retains a larger average depth than both the [[North Sea]] and [[Baltic Sea]] respectively.

[[Thomas Robert Malthus]] traveled through Norway in 1799 and his diaries from the trip includes a description of Mjøsa. Malthus wrote that Mjøsa appears as both lake and river because the shores are defined by mountains and where the valley becomes wider the water fills the space. Below Minde ([[Minnesund]]) the lake only appears like a river and is called [[Vorma]] on the map, according to Malthus.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Den Store Mjøsboka |publisher=Pegasus |year=1991 |editor-last=Selstad |editor-first=Tor |location=Oslo |editor-last2=Stensrud |editor-first2=Arve}}</ref>

==Location==
[[file:la2-demis-mjosa.png|left|thumb|Map with cities [[Lillehammer (town)|Lillehammer]] (North), [[Gjøvik (town)|Gjøvik]] (West), and [[Hamar]] (East)]]
From its southernmost point at Minnesund in [[Eidsvoll Municipality]] to its northernmost point in [[Lillehammer Municipality]], it is {{convert|117|km}} long.<ref name="dybdekart" /> At its widest, near the town of [[Hamar]], it is {{convert|9|km}} wide.<ref name="dybdekart" /> It is {{convert|369|km2}}<ref name="dybdekart" /> in area and its volume is estimated at {{convert|56|km3|cumi|abbr=on}}; normally its surface is about {{convert|121-123|m|ft|abbr=on}} above sea level, and its greatest depth is {{convert|453|m|ft|abbr =on}}.<ref name="dybdekart" /> Its total coastline is estimated at {{convert|341|km|mi|abbr=on}}, of which 30% is built up. Dams built on the [[distributary]] of Vorma in 1858, 1911, 1947, and 1965 raised the level by approximately {{convert|3.6|m|ft|abbr=on}} in total. During the 19th and 20th centuries, there have been 20 floods recorded that added at least {{convert|7|m|ft|abbr=on}} to the level of the lake which caused flooding. Several of these floods inundated the city of [[Hamar]].

The towns of [[Hamar]], [[Gjøvik (town)|Gjøvik]], and [[Lillehammer (town)|Lillehammer]] were founded along the shores of the lake. Before the construction of railways past the lake, it was an important transport route. Today, aside from minor leisure boating and the steamship ''[[Skibladner]]'', there is no water traffic on the lake. Most of its shores are dominated by rolling agricultural areas, among them some of the most fertile grainlands in Norway. The main train line, the [[Dovre Line]] between [[Oslo]] and [[Trondheim]], goes along its eastern shore, making stops at [[Hamar Station]] and [[Lillehammer Station]]. From the south, the [[European route E6]] highway runs along the eastern shore of the lake until the [[Mjøsa Bridge]] connects [[Moelv]] on the east with [[Biri, Norway|Biri]] on the west.

==Mjøskastellet==
[[File:Mjøstkastellet Peter Blix 1897.gif|thumb|left|Drawing of Mjøstkastellet as imagined by  Peter Andreas Blix]]
The largest and only island in the lake is [[Helgøya, Hedmark|Helgøya]]. Except for Helgøya, Mjøsa only contains small islets. The most interesting of these is Steinsholmen, which holds the ruins of Mjøskastellet, a [[medieval]] [[citadel]] dating from the 13th century. Established by King [[Haakon IV of Norway]], it was first mentioned historically in a letter dated 1234. [[Peter Andreas Blix]] documented the site and made drawings in 1897. [[Hedmark Museum]] has a future archaeological plan for the site.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mjøskastellet på Steinsholmen |url=http://www.mjossamlingene.no/index.php/mjoshistorie/93-mjoskastellet-pa-steinsholmen |access-date=2017-06-01 |publisher=Mjøssamlingene}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Mjøskastellet |url=https://snl.no/Mj%C3%B8skastellet |access-date=2017-06-01 |publisher=Store norske leksikon}}</ref>

==Fish==
Lake Mjøsa has 20 species of fish. Among the most common are [[Pike (fish)|pike]], [[European perch]], [[common roach]], [[Thymallus thymallus|greyling]], and the hundertrout, a [[brown trout]] which can reach a weight more than {{convert|20|kg}}. Another common species is the [[European smelt]], which is the most important [[bait fish]] for the predators. Historically, the most economically significant species is the ''[[Coregonus albula|lågsild]]'' (European cisco).

== Source of drinking water ==
Several municipal and urban areas use the lake as their source of drinking water.
*[[Hamar]] / [[Ottestad]] – Hias IKS has responsibility for the [[waterworks]] and [[waste water treatment plants]] in [[Hamar Municipality]], [[Stange Municipality]], and [[Løten Municipality]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Om Hias |url=https://www.hias.no/om-hias |language=no}}</ref>
*[[Lillehammer Municipality]] –  Operates waterworks at Korgen. Contracts have been signed for a new flood-proof waterworks to be built in Hovemoen.
*[[Gjøvik Municipality]] – WWTP run from Rambekk Renseanlegg
*[[Østre Toten Municipality]] – Kapp / Skreia
*[[Brumunddal]] – Biri / Moelv

==Name==
The lake is named "Mjøsa" ({{Langx|non|Mjǫrs}} / [[Proto-Norse]] ''*Merso'') and this name must be very old. The meaning is possibly "the bright/shiny one". This is based on the theory that the first element ''*mer-'' is related to the English word ''mere'' which now means "just" or "only", but formerly also meant "clear" or "bright". The second element ''-so'' would be a [[suffix]].{{cn|date=November 2025}}. However, ''mere'' in this sense came into use in Middle English from Latin via French rather than from its Germanic Old English roots, making this meaning unlikely.

